## Migration: `EventProcessor` → .NET 8

### Problems in the Original Code

| API | Issue on .NET 8 |
|-----|-----------------|
| `Thread.Abort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` (SYSLIB0006) |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | Obsolete (SYSLIB0046 in .NET 9), unreliable wake mechanism |
| `ManualResetEvent.WaitOne()` | Not cancellation-aware — can't be interrupted cooperatively |
| Race condition | Multiple enqueues with one `Reset()` can lose signals |

### Patterns Identified

1. **Cancellable work loop** — `while(true)` with `WaitOne()`
2. **Blocking call interruption** — `Interrupt()` to wake from `WaitOne()`
3. **Uncooperative termination** — `Abort()` as fallback

---

### Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
using System.Threading.Channels;

public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly Channel<string> _channel = Channel.CreateUnbounded<string>();
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private Task? _runTask;

    public void Start()
    {
        _runTask = Task.Run(() => RunLoopAsync(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task RunLoopAsync(CancellationToken token)
    {
        try
        {
            await foreach (var item in _channel.Reader.ReadAllAsync(token))
            {
                Handle(item);
            }
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            // Normal shutdown — nothing to do
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        _channel.Writer.TryWrite(item);
    }

    public async Task StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        _channel.Writer.Complete(); // Signal no more items
        timeout ??= TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);

        // Give the loop time to drain
        if (_runTask is not null)
        {
            using var timeoutCts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout.Value);
            try
            {
                await _runTask.WaitAsync(timeoutCts.Token);
            }
            catch (TimeoutException)
            {
                // Loop didn't finish in time — force cancellation
                await _cts.CancelAsync();
                await _runTask.WaitAsync(CancellationToken.None);
            }
        }
    }

    private void Handle(string item) { /* your logic */ }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        _cts.Cancel();
        _cts.Dispose();
    }
}
```

---

### Key Pitfalls to Watch

| Pitfall | Mitigation |
|---------|-----------|
| `Thread.Abort` → `PlatformNotSupportedException` | Replaced with `CancellationToken` + `Channel.Complete()` |
| Original race condition (multiple enqueues, one signal) | `Channel<T>` handles producer/consumer atomically — no lost items |
| Forgetting to dispose `CancellationTokenSource` | `IDisposable` pattern ensures cleanup |
| Blocking `Stop()` in sync context | Provide sync wrapper if needed: `StopAsync().GetAwaiter().GetResult()` |
| `Handle()` ignoring cancellation | If `Handle` is long-running, pass `token` into it and check periodically |

### If You Must Keep a Synchronous API

```csharp
// Synchronous alternative using BlockingCollection (no async)
public class EventProcessorSync : IDisposable
{
    private readonly BlockingCollection<string> _queue = new();
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private Thread? _thread;

    public void Start()
    {
        _thread = new Thread(RunLoop) { IsBackground = true };
        _thread.Start();
    }

    private void RunLoop()
    {
        try
        {
            foreach (var item in _queue.GetConsumingEnumerable(_cts.Token))
                Handle(item);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) { }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item) => _queue.Add(item);

    public void Stop()
    {
        _queue.CompleteAdding();       // graceful drain
        if (!_thread!.Join(5000))
        {
            _cts.Cancel();             // force cancellation
            _thread.Join();            // now it exits promptly
        }
    }

    private void Handle(string item) { }
    public void Dispose() { _cts.Cancel(); _cts.Dispose(); _queue.Dispose(); }
}
```

### Summary

- **Prefer `Channel<T>`** (async) or **`BlockingCollection<T>`** (sync) — both eliminate the manual lock + event + queue pattern and fix the race condition.
- **Replace `Thread.Abort/Interrupt`** with `CancellationToken` — the only supported model in .NET 8.
- **Two-phase shutdown**: signal completion first (drain gracefully), then cancel if timeout expires.
